WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.090
- I'm so happy you guys are enjoying

00:00:02.090 --> 00:00:03.590
the Doctor Reacts series.

00:00:03.590 --> 00:00:05.650
Please subscribe if you're
enjoying the videos.

00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:07.046
I jumped into the comments section

00:00:07.046 --> 00:00:09.190
and I hear you loud and clear.

00:00:09.190 --> 00:00:11.088
Code Black is up next.

00:00:11.088 --> 00:00:14.171
(rhythmic pop music)

00:00:17.214 --> 00:00:18.850
(static)

00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:20.969
An influx of patients so great,

00:00:20.969 --> 00:00:23.150
At the different hospitals that I worked

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:26.220
there were different definitions of code

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:27.450
followed by a different color.

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:29.950
For example Code Blue generally meant

00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:32.760
that someone's heart stopped
or they weren't breathing.

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:34.300
Rapid response meant something.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:37.467
Code Pink sometimes meant
that a baby was abducted

00:00:37.467 --> 00:00:39.480
from the newborn nursery.

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:43.480
Code Black generally referred
to like a terrorist threat

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:45.440
or some kind of crime going on.

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:47.840
I've never heard this type
of definition from this show

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:49.620
but, it does make sense.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:52.090
Hospitals get overcrowded,
especially in busy areas

00:00:52.090 --> 00:00:52.960
like Los Angeles.

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:54.150
I'm excited to watch this.

00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:58.090
- Your momma know when you're
lying, crying, or dying

00:00:58.090 --> 00:01:00.750
and it's not only my job to
teach you right from wrong,

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:03.760
but to make sure you don't
leave here in three years

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:06.830
thinking you can do no wrong.

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:09.360
- One of the biggest things
you have to do as a doctor

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:11.640
is learn that there's
a lot you can do wrong.

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:15.160
I'm talking about over treating,
over diagnosing patients.

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:17.470
That's truly a health
hazard that most people

00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:18.620
don't even think about.

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:22.080
So, I love this nurse and
frankly I love this actor,

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:24.060
so I'm super excited to
see him in this show.

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:27.020
- The most famous strip
of hospital real estate

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:29.695
in the nation, where
emergency medicine was born,

00:01:29.695 --> 00:01:32.690
where the secret service
reserves a trauma bed

00:01:32.690 --> 00:01:35.020
for the President of the United States

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:36.733
when he is in Los Angeles.

00:01:36.733 --> 00:01:37.885
This is trauma one.

00:01:37.885 --> 00:01:38.718
- Wow.

00:01:38.718 --> 00:01:40.060
- Angels Memorial.

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:44.760
You are not under any
circumstances allowed

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:47.863
to kill a guest in my house.

00:01:51.512 --> 00:01:52.660
- I love this orientation,
this is so good.

00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:54.150
- What the hell is the homeboy drop off?

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:56.050
- Gang bangers leave the
wounded on the sidewalk.

00:01:56.050 --> 00:02:00.690
- I've worked in numerous
hospitals uh where the ER was

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:03.020
in a part of town where there was a lot

00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:04.750
of gang related activities

00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:07.540
and literally people would
walk into the hospital

00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:10.010
or get thrown out of a
car with a gunshot wound

00:02:10.010 --> 00:02:13.120
and there was one time I
literally got a patient

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:15.760
that crawled into the uh emergency room

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:17.620
bleeding from his femoral artery,

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:20.090
so I had to grab a glove, stick my finger

00:02:20.090 --> 00:02:23.981
into his femoral artery and
pull him to the bed and pull him

00:02:23.981 --> 00:02:25.120
to the bed and get the
whole process started

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:25.970
with the trauma team.

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:29.114
I remember that because my
hands were so covered uh

00:02:29.114 --> 00:02:33.820
in blood, at least up to my
uh elbows, that I was like

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:35.840
oh man, I have to wash
this off really quickly

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:38.160
cause that's the first step
of infection control is

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:39.360
to wash your hands.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:43.590
I left the trauma scene and
the trauma bay and walked

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:47.120
to one of the nearest um sinks
to wash my hands properly

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:49.350
because the situation
was being taken care of

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:51.810
by a lot of doctors, and then I realized

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:54.094
I was tracking blood
all across the hospital,

00:02:54.094 --> 00:02:57.560
so the maintenance staff looked
at me with these angry eyes

00:02:57.560 --> 00:03:00.040
and I was like I'm so sorry,
I didn't mean to make a mess,

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:03.090
but literally they had to
clean up my bloody footsteps

00:03:03.090 --> 00:03:04.990
that traveled halfway across the hospital

00:03:04.990 --> 00:03:06.030
to go wash my hands.

00:03:06.030 --> 00:03:08.450
It's part of life, it's
scary but it's part of life.

00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:11.250
- Gunshot wound to the
neck, obliterated carotid.

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:12.495
He's lost half his volume here, Jess,

00:03:12.495 --> 00:03:13.735
we got to get him out of here
- Oh my god, that's dangerous

00:03:13.735 --> 00:03:14.970
- Just get the gurney
underneath him please.

00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:16.270
- Alright, you get over
there on the other side

00:03:16.270 --> 00:03:17.362
and push with both legs.

00:03:17.362 --> 00:03:19.667
One, two, three, go.

00:03:21.987 --> 00:03:23.410
- So this is very realistic.

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:25.030
I know it looks like they're being rough

00:03:25.030 --> 00:03:27.276
and they're kicking the
patient out of the uh SUV,

00:03:27.276 --> 00:03:29.692
but that's true because
you literally have uh

00:03:29.692 --> 00:03:33.620
seconds before the person
completely bleeds out and dies,

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:35.110
especially from the carotid artery.

00:03:35.110 --> 00:03:37.660
Plugging the wound
obviously is the first step,

00:03:37.660 --> 00:03:39.320
so they're doing a great job.

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:40.840
I'm assuming they still feel a pulse

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:43.130
cause they said that the
artery was still wiggling.

00:03:43.130 --> 00:03:44.870
So right now, the most important thing

00:03:44.870 --> 00:03:47.280
is to fluid resuscitate this person,

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:49.640
meaning to give as much fluids as you can

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:51.510
with two large bore ivs.

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:54.040
Large bore meaning thick ivs
so they can deliver a lot

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:55.300
of fluids very quickly

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:58.500
and then it's to obviously
give them uh blood back,

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:00.290
donated blood that we have in the hospital

00:04:00.290 --> 00:04:03.110
from the blood bank, and give
them as much blood as we can

00:04:03.110 --> 00:04:04.299
in the short period of time.

00:04:04.299 --> 00:04:05.420
- Look at his pulse, doctor,

00:04:05.420 --> 00:04:07.218
this man's heart need's assistance.

00:04:07.218 --> 00:04:08.560
- Stop compressing til we can intubate.

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:10.077
- Got it?

00:04:10.077 --> 00:04:10.910
- Yeah.

00:04:10.910 --> 00:04:12.350
- Who can tell me why this
patient can not be transfused

00:04:12.350 --> 00:04:14.950
in type and crossmatched blood? Anyone?

00:04:14.950 --> 00:04:18.060
- Because it takes time to
type and cross someone's blood,

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:19.800
so therefore you want to give them blood

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:22.190
that's universally considered donor blood,

00:04:22.190 --> 00:04:23.710
which is O negative blood.

00:04:23.710 --> 00:04:25.200
- I need a hero, come on.

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:28.680
Come on, let's go find
you an attending to annoy.

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:30.840
- Honestly, this is so accurate,

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:33.208
because at times the hospital's busy

00:04:33.208 --> 00:04:37.160
you have to find a doctor
to shadow or learn from

00:04:37.160 --> 00:04:40.420
or follow around and it is
sometimes unorganized like this,

00:04:40.420 --> 00:04:41.660
especially when it gets busy.

00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:42.510
- What's your name?

00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:43.343
- Ariel.

00:04:43.343 --> 00:04:44.357
- Ariel, I'm Dr. Rorish

00:04:44.357 --> 00:04:46.900
and the truth is we don't know
why your father's unconscious

00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:48.630
and I can't learn anything
out here in the dark.

00:04:48.630 --> 00:04:51.470
So with your permission,
I want to take him inside

00:04:51.470 --> 00:04:53.160
and I promise you I will
tell you everything I know

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:55.452
as soon as I know it, okay?

00:04:55.452 --> 00:04:56.285
- Okay.

00:04:56.285 --> 00:04:58.220
- Very good doctor-patient relationship.

00:04:58.220 --> 00:05:02.230
I found myself very often
in the emergency room

00:05:02.230 --> 00:05:04.440
having very serious
conversations like that

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:06.068
and I found that yields the best results,

00:05:06.068 --> 00:05:08.760
so even in dire situations

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:10.445
when a family is hysterical like that,

00:05:10.445 --> 00:05:12.324
the more honest I was with them,

00:05:12.324 --> 00:05:14.328
and I was brutally honest at times,

00:05:14.328 --> 00:05:17.230
saying that these treatments
aren't going to help

00:05:17.230 --> 00:05:19.220
your father, mother,
whoever I was talking about

00:05:19.220 --> 00:05:21.316
at the time, and I would apologize,

00:05:21.316 --> 00:05:23.550
I would say I'm sorry I'm being
brutally honest right now,

00:05:23.550 --> 00:05:25.151
but I want you to make the best decision

00:05:25.151 --> 00:05:27.009
with the limited amount of time we have

00:05:27.009 --> 00:05:28.612
and people respected that,
they appreciated that

00:05:28.612 --> 00:05:31.940
because in reality nobody
wants them to lie to them,

00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:33.870
nobody wants false promises.

00:05:33.870 --> 00:05:35.058
- What's your evaluation, doctor?

00:05:35.058 --> 00:05:38.430
- Patient unconscious,
low bp, warm extremities

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:39.800
and he's in spinal shock.

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:41.030
- No additional examination.

00:05:41.030 --> 00:05:42.425
- Uh, it's pretty textbook.

00:05:42.425 --> 00:05:43.480
- Pretty textbook, huh?

00:05:43.480 --> 00:05:44.313
- Yeah.

00:05:44.313 --> 00:05:45.601
- Okay, what about you doctor, textbook?

00:05:45.601 --> 00:05:48.240
- The way that this attending is teaching,

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:50.680
while trying to actively
save the patient's life

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:52.260
is very accurate.

00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:54.920
Like I feel like I'm in the
hospital as a resident learning.

00:05:54.920 --> 00:05:57.863
It's very accurate, this
is exactly how it happens.

00:05:58.720 --> 00:05:59.890
She's not giving the patient worse care,

00:05:59.890 --> 00:06:02.520
which is also educating with pressure

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:04.528
in a time sensitive
situation to the residents.

00:06:04.528 --> 00:06:07.250
Excellent, excellent recreation.

00:06:07.250 --> 00:06:10.143
- 654 is normal, brain
function 111 is the lowest.

00:06:10.143 --> 00:06:11.723
- He's brain dead.

00:06:13.590 --> 00:06:14.510
- That poor kid.

00:06:14.510 --> 00:06:17.150
- He was confused that
I didn't understand him.

00:06:17.150 --> 00:06:19.587
- Yes, called expressive aphasia.

00:06:19.587 --> 00:06:22.670
It's word salad, sounds
right to him but the words

00:06:22.670 --> 00:06:24.100
come out all tossed around.

00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:26.190
- Yeah, expressive aphasia
is really interesting,

00:06:26.190 --> 00:06:28.820
it's also known as Broca's aphasia

00:06:28.820 --> 00:06:31.700
and that happens if you have
damage to the left front uh

00:06:31.700 --> 00:06:33.300
frontal area of your brain,

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:35.050
so that can happen in car accidents,

00:06:35.050 --> 00:06:37.350
it can happen in traumatic illnesses,

00:06:37.350 --> 00:06:38.549
also strokes, basically what happens is,

00:06:38.549 --> 00:06:41.600
in your mind you know what you want to say

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:43.890
and it's you think you're
saying the right words

00:06:43.890 --> 00:06:44.723
but when it comes out

00:06:44.723 --> 00:06:46.670
you're saying completely different words

00:06:46.670 --> 00:06:48.900
and the way that I memorized
this when I was studying

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:52.580
and learning about it was
boca means mouth in Spanish

00:06:52.580 --> 00:06:54.860
so it was like what's coming
out of your mouth is wrong.

00:06:54.860 --> 00:06:58.330
- TPA is a kind of miracle
drug that has the potential

00:06:58.330 --> 00:07:00.312
to bust the clot up
and reverse the stroke,

00:07:00.312 --> 00:07:03.330
but only if there's no actual
bleeding yet in the brain.

00:07:03.330 --> 00:07:05.330
We're going to need a
scan to know for sure.

00:07:05.330 --> 00:07:07.260
- So this is the one part about this

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:08.864
that's pretty inaccurate.

00:07:08.864 --> 00:07:10.943
So, most hospitals have stroke protocols

00:07:10.943 --> 00:07:15.210
when a patient even comes in
with remotely similar symptoms

00:07:15.210 --> 00:07:17.526
to a stroke they instantly
go to a CAT scan,

00:07:17.526 --> 00:07:19.586
they instantly get blood work drawn,

00:07:19.586 --> 00:07:21.930
you instantly page the internal medicine

00:07:21.930 --> 00:07:24.408
or family medicine residents
to follow stroke protocol,

00:07:24.408 --> 00:07:27.393
we instantly call the
neurologist and get the robot out

00:07:27.393 --> 00:07:29.870
and all of this happens
right away because again

00:07:29.870 --> 00:07:31.740
you have a small time of window

00:07:32.685 --> 00:07:33.540
that you could actually give the TPA.

00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:35.620
So it's about the fastest fastest way

00:07:35.620 --> 00:07:36.800
you can get things done.

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:39.990
Never is it standing at
bedside having conversations

00:07:39.990 --> 00:07:42.620
of oh, let's get the CT scan

00:07:42.620 --> 00:07:44.460
and see if there's any
bleeding going on. No.

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:45.710
- He's brain dead.

00:07:45.710 --> 00:07:47.300
- Oh, god.

00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:48.901
- And she's all alone til tomorrow.

00:07:48.901 --> 00:07:51.330
- Has someone called
child and family services

00:07:51.330 --> 00:07:52.163
to come get her?

00:07:52.163 --> 00:07:54.123
- Yeah, but you know how
that goes it could be hours.

00:07:56.010 --> 00:07:58.453
- It gets worse, he's an organ donor.

00:07:59.460 --> 00:08:00.970
Transplant team's on the way.

00:08:00.970 --> 00:08:02.920
- Then we can't wait for
CFS, I have to tell her.

00:08:02.920 --> 00:08:05.945
- This is definitely the
most medically accurate show

00:08:05.945 --> 00:08:08.062
I've seen in such a long time.

00:08:08.062 --> 00:08:10.560
I don't know why I'm
getting excited about it

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:12.210
but it's just it's very accurate.

00:08:12.210 --> 00:08:13.940
These conversations happen in this matter.

00:08:13.940 --> 00:08:15.994
- Your father died, Ariel.

00:08:15.994 --> 00:08:20.994
- Well, technically, technically
it's uh a brain death.

00:08:21.250 --> 00:08:22.420
- So he's not dead?

00:08:22.420 --> 00:08:24.637
- No, no. That's, what I mean is

00:08:24.637 --> 00:08:27.236
machines are keeping
his body at a state of

00:08:27.236 --> 00:08:28.930
- What are you saying?

00:08:28.930 --> 00:08:29.864
- I'm trying to explain to you

00:08:29.864 --> 00:08:30.965
- No you're not.

00:08:30.965 --> 00:08:32.081
What happened to just
giving it to me straight?

00:08:32.081 --> 00:08:33.270
- I'm giving it to you straight.

00:08:33.270 --> 00:08:35.243
- So he's dead, but
technically not really, right?

00:08:35.243 --> 00:08:37.060
Oh, I want to go see
him, I want to see him

00:08:37.060 --> 00:08:38.780
- I've had conversations like this,

00:08:38.780 --> 00:08:40.511
again the more brutally honest you can be,

00:08:40.511 --> 00:08:44.010
at least in the beginning,
that's usually the best policy.

00:08:44.010 --> 00:08:46.499
My general strategy is to get
as much of the information

00:08:46.499 --> 00:08:48.916
out there, console them as much as I can,

00:08:48.916 --> 00:08:51.340
but if they become hysterical, get upset,

00:08:51.340 --> 00:08:54.920
tell you to shut up, tell you
to leave, never get offended

00:08:54.920 --> 00:08:57.170
because what they say in those moments

00:08:57.170 --> 00:08:58.274
that's not who they are as people,

00:08:58.274 --> 00:09:01.180
they're going through a moment
of shock, emotional shock.

00:09:01.180 --> 00:09:02.720
I'll leave them alone in the room

00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:04.890
with maybe someone watching
them a nurse or someone else

00:09:04.890 --> 00:09:07.046
but me because I'm the
one who gave the bad news,

00:09:07.046 --> 00:09:08.686
give them some time,

00:09:08.686 --> 00:09:11.850
and then come see them
once some time has gone by

00:09:11.850 --> 00:09:14.620
and pretty much every time that
I've done this conversation

00:09:14.620 --> 00:09:16.724
they apologize, we hug,

00:09:16.724 --> 00:09:19.660
I understand that they've
just lost a family member,

00:09:19.660 --> 00:09:22.250
I've gone through that
in my life with my mom.

00:09:22.250 --> 00:09:24.800
I've actually had to ask
residents to stop doing CPR

00:09:24.800 --> 00:09:27.247
on my mom, so I know what this feels like

00:09:27.247 --> 00:09:31.040
and sometimes people just
need a moment to themselves,

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:32.670
they need to not see the person

00:09:32.670 --> 00:09:34.310
who just gave them the bad news,

00:09:34.310 --> 00:09:37.410
so it's okay to be the bad
person, leave, come back.

00:09:37.410 --> 00:09:38.883
- Transplant team's in route.

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:43.173
I want to tell you how
fabulous you look right now.

00:09:43.173 --> 00:09:44.370
- I do?

00:09:44.370 --> 00:09:46.377
- No, I want to tell you
that but then it'd be a lie.

00:09:46.377 --> 00:09:47.210
(laughter)

00:09:47.210 --> 00:09:49.181
- You look like you've been
strapped to the bow of a ship.

00:09:49.181 --> 00:09:51.410
- So true, um, this is so true
- [Dr. Rorish] Is that so?

00:09:51.410 --> 00:09:53.890
- Interesting about the transplant team.

00:09:53.890 --> 00:09:56.960
They are a skilled set
of surgeons that come in

00:09:56.960 --> 00:09:58.221
and basically take the organs

00:09:58.221 --> 00:10:00.731
as soon as the time of death is called.

00:10:00.731 --> 00:10:04.990
Actually, um while I
was doing my training uh

00:10:04.990 --> 00:10:08.186
when I was on the surgical
unit I was one of the people

00:10:08.186 --> 00:10:11.550
that was put in a room
with the transplant team

00:10:11.550 --> 00:10:12.870
to call the time of death.

00:10:12.870 --> 00:10:15.025
You can not start to
operate on the patient

00:10:15.025 --> 00:10:18.910
taking out the organs, harvesting
the organs, what have you,

00:10:18.910 --> 00:10:21.100
until the person is completely dead,

00:10:21.100 --> 00:10:24.202
so we had to stand there
until I didn't feel a pulse

00:10:24.202 --> 00:10:25.900
and my exam was negative.

00:10:25.900 --> 00:10:28.470
There's a lot of pressure
and there there was a time

00:10:28.470 --> 00:10:30.560
where there wasn't a
pulse then he came back

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:31.953
so I had to say well you know, wait,

00:10:31.953 --> 00:10:34.570
and it was a lot of pressure
on me being a young doctor,

00:10:34.570 --> 00:10:36.630
but I wanted to do what
was right by the patient

00:10:36.630 --> 00:10:39.010
and waited until the
heart completely stopped,

00:10:39.010 --> 00:10:40.670
the patient had no pain response,

00:10:40.670 --> 00:10:43.370
and only then did we begin
uh taking out the organs

00:10:43.370 --> 00:10:44.810
so they could save someone else's life.

00:10:44.810 --> 00:10:46.277
- Oh no, don't do that.

00:10:46.277 --> 00:10:48.737
You don't need anymore
laugh lines, believe me.

00:10:48.737 --> 00:10:50.318
- Me, have you looked at
yourself in the mirror lately?

00:10:50.318 --> 00:10:51.151
- Uh huh.

00:10:53.640 --> 00:10:55.187
- I love their relationship, it's so real

00:10:55.187 --> 00:10:56.630
- I love you so much, I
want to break you in half.

00:10:56.630 --> 00:10:59.692
- I love you so much, I
want to punch your face in.

00:10:59.692 --> 00:11:00.525
(laughter)

00:11:00.525 --> 00:11:02.750
- There was a recent article
whether or not it's okay

00:11:02.750 --> 00:11:04.742
for doctors to hug patients
and other coworkers

00:11:04.742 --> 00:11:07.430
and I thought it was insane
because if we're going to get

00:11:07.430 --> 00:11:09.520
to the state of where we're not allowed

00:11:09.520 --> 00:11:11.330
to make physical contact with people,

00:11:11.330 --> 00:11:13.136
humans crave physical contact.

00:11:13.136 --> 00:11:15.253
I'm not talking about
inappropriate physical contact,

00:11:15.253 --> 00:11:16.930
I'm talking about moments like that

00:11:16.930 --> 00:11:18.950
where you need encouragement,
where you need a hug.

00:11:18.950 --> 00:11:20.104
Even most recently I had like

00:11:20.104 --> 00:11:22.490
an eighty-four year old patient

00:11:22.490 --> 00:11:24.180
that was going through a
difficult moment in her life

00:11:24.180 --> 00:11:25.852
and I spent an hour with her in a visit,

00:11:25.852 --> 00:11:27.760
we talked about all the things going on,

00:11:27.760 --> 00:11:29.170
we treated her medical conditions,

00:11:29.170 --> 00:11:31.500
she became very emotional and
cried and asked for a hug.

00:11:31.500 --> 00:11:33.010
Of course I'm going to hug her.

00:11:33.010 --> 00:11:34.540
- Worst part is the headache.

00:11:34.540 --> 00:11:36.040
I live above the boiler
room in my building

00:11:36.040 --> 00:11:37.391
and the noise just keeps
cracking my head open.

00:11:37.391 --> 00:11:39.420
Is there anything you
can give me for that?

00:11:39.420 --> 00:11:40.370
- You're nine months pregnant,

00:11:40.370 --> 00:11:41.340
there's not a whole lot we can do

00:11:41.340 --> 00:11:42.590
other than give you Tylenol.

00:11:42.590 --> 00:11:45.110
- I've tried that, it doesn't work.

00:11:45.110 --> 00:11:46.630
- Using again?

00:11:46.630 --> 00:11:47.463
- Excuse me?

00:11:47.463 --> 00:11:48.296
- Mario.

00:11:48.296 --> 00:11:49.540
- Scars on the inside of
your arm are all track marks,

00:11:49.540 --> 00:11:50.440
right?

00:11:50.440 --> 00:11:51.830
- None of your damn business.

00:11:51.830 --> 00:11:54.100
- Bad bedside manor obviously.

00:11:54.100 --> 00:11:56.630
I've never seen a more aggressive resident

00:11:56.630 --> 00:11:58.119
in in questioning a patient.

00:11:58.119 --> 00:11:59.416
You're going to get the patient angry,

00:11:59.416 --> 00:12:01.264
whether not you're right or wrong,

00:12:01.264 --> 00:12:03.876
it could bring up old
feelings or old habits

00:12:03.876 --> 00:12:06.572
that the patient had that
can make them very emotional,

00:12:06.572 --> 00:12:08.050
they're going to disconnect,

00:12:08.050 --> 00:12:09.262
they're not going to
follow what you're saying.

00:12:09.262 --> 00:12:11.432
No one's winning here by you doing that.

00:12:11.432 --> 00:12:12.497
Shame on you, sir.

00:12:12.497 --> 00:12:13.672
- Uh, where are you from?

00:12:13.672 --> 00:12:14.505
- Norway.

00:12:14.505 --> 00:12:15.338
- Uh, center stage.

00:12:15.338 --> 00:12:18.080
Your son has a collapsed
lung we're going to help him.

00:12:18.080 --> 00:12:18.913
- How could you know that?

00:12:18.913 --> 00:12:20.719
- That the Norwegian kid can't breathe,

00:12:20.719 --> 00:12:23.191
it's pneumothorax it's
a genetic disposition.

00:12:23.191 --> 00:12:24.615
- I've never heard of that.

00:12:24.615 --> 00:12:26.820
(coughing)

00:12:26.820 --> 00:12:30.200
I'm about to have
spontaneous pneumothorax.

00:12:30.200 --> 00:12:33.028
- A Norwegian child having pneumothorax?

00:12:33.028 --> 00:12:34.850
I'm learning something from this show.

00:12:34.850 --> 00:12:36.350
I'm going to look this up afterwards.

00:12:36.350 --> 00:12:37.970
Actually, let's look this
up right now together.

00:12:37.970 --> 00:12:39.560
I'm curious what what pops up here.

00:12:39.560 --> 00:12:41.040
A significant fraction of families

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:42.740
with familial spontaneous pneumothorax

00:12:42.740 --> 00:12:44.290
have mutations in the gene.

00:12:44.290 --> 00:12:45.660
The fact that she's just guessing

00:12:45.660 --> 00:12:48.815
that this child has pneumothorax
without a physical exam

00:12:48.815 --> 00:12:50.350
is a little crazy for me.

00:12:50.350 --> 00:12:51.986
- Diesel fumes. Oh my god.

00:12:51.986 --> 00:12:52.819
- What?

00:12:52.819 --> 00:12:53.757
- What if it's causing carbon monoxide

00:12:53.757 --> 00:12:54.930
- She lives on top of a boiler room

00:12:54.930 --> 00:12:57.350
- Carbon monoxide poisoning
can mimic flu symptoms.

00:12:57.350 --> 00:12:59.210
She said she lived above
a boiler room, right?

00:12:59.210 --> 00:13:01.640
Oh my god, Mario I could kiss you.

00:13:01.640 --> 00:13:02.580
But not really.

00:13:02.580 --> 00:13:05.110
- Common symptoms of
carbon monoxide poisoning

00:13:05.110 --> 00:13:08.650
is feeling sleepy, having
a headache, fatigue,

00:13:08.650 --> 00:13:12.790
and the treatment is breathing in oxygen.

00:13:12.790 --> 00:13:14.365
- You're not going to
like this Neal, but uh

00:13:14.365 --> 00:13:15.900
- She has to perform a C-section on a rig.

00:13:15.900 --> 00:13:17.260
Sometimes you have to be a cowboy, right?

00:13:17.260 --> 00:13:18.093
- Oh god.

00:13:18.991 --> 00:13:20.242
(water bursting)

00:13:20.242 --> 00:13:21.075
Oh. Okay.

00:13:21.075 --> 00:13:22.873
- Now, this is going
to happen very quickly.

00:13:22.873 --> 00:13:26.410
I want you to reach your hand
inside as fast as you can,

00:13:26.410 --> 00:13:28.000
carefully feel for the baby.

00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:28.833
- When?

00:13:28.833 --> 00:13:29.666
- Have you made the cut yet?

00:13:29.666 --> 00:13:30.499
- Yes.

00:13:30.499 --> 00:13:32.000
- Now.
- Now.

00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:33.950
- These are very crude instructions,

00:13:33.950 --> 00:13:35.650
but I guess in an emergence setting

00:13:35.650 --> 00:13:36.850
this is what they need to do.

00:13:36.850 --> 00:13:39.890
It feels like they're making
a rash decision here uh

00:13:39.890 --> 00:13:41.770
in doing a C-section like this

00:13:41.770 --> 00:13:43.444
with someone who's not a trained operator.

00:13:43.444 --> 00:13:45.833
There's so many mistakes that can happen.

00:13:46.740 --> 00:13:48.073
I don't know.

00:13:48.073 --> 00:13:50.423
I don't know what the right answer is.

00:13:52.076 --> 00:13:53.010
- Her, her.

00:13:53.010 --> 00:13:54.976
(baby crying)

00:13:54.976 --> 00:13:56.410
I got her.

00:13:56.410 --> 00:13:57.782
(baby crying)

00:13:57.782 --> 00:13:59.510
- How about suturing the patient back up

00:13:59.510 --> 00:14:01.010
so she doesn't bleed to death.

00:14:02.060 --> 00:14:04.513
- Hi, I'm Vanessa.

00:14:05.585 --> 00:14:06.418
- I'm Ariel.

00:14:06.418 --> 00:14:08.990
- Oh, the organs were used to save her.

00:14:08.990 --> 00:14:09.890
- Can you hear it?

00:14:12.720 --> 00:14:16.820
- Yeah, I can hear it.

00:14:16.820 --> 00:14:19.335
- Powerful moment, when you know uh

00:14:19.335 --> 00:14:22.010
you had to lose your
father but it saved uh

00:14:22.010 --> 00:14:23.280
another person's life.

00:14:23.280 --> 00:14:25.320
Code Black, in the books.

00:14:25.320 --> 00:14:28.010
This show pleasantly surprised me.

00:14:28.010 --> 00:14:30.770
I feel like all the other
shows I've watched so far

00:14:30.770 --> 00:14:33.920
were written by a Hollywood person

00:14:33.920 --> 00:14:37.160
and then a doctor contributed
some medical knowledge to it

00:14:37.160 --> 00:14:40.430
as opposed to this one, it
felt like it was written

00:14:40.430 --> 00:14:42.900
by a doctor and then a Hollywood person

00:14:42.900 --> 00:14:44.067
injected some drama into it.

00:14:44.067 --> 00:14:46.820
It was a good show. I
mean it was feel good,

00:14:46.820 --> 00:14:49.540
it was medically accurate
where it needed to be,

00:14:49.540 --> 00:14:52.130
the interpersonal
relationships were accurate.

00:14:52.130 --> 00:14:54.710
I'm excited for this show, I
think it has a lot of potential

00:14:54.710 --> 00:14:57.390
and if you guys like this
show please let me know,

00:14:57.390 --> 00:15:00.400
give me some episodes to
watch because I'm so down

00:15:00.400 --> 00:15:02.550
to watch more of Code Black.

00:15:02.550 --> 00:15:04.430
Go ahead and smash that subscribe

00:15:04.430 --> 00:15:06.150
and like button down below.

00:15:06.150 --> 00:15:08.510
As always, stay happy and healthy.

00:15:08.510 --> 00:15:11.593
(rhythmic pop music)

